The D&D adventure board games (Castle Ravenloft and Wrath of
Ashardalon) would be a fantastic template on how to do a Nethack
tabletop game. The dungeons are randomly generated based on tile
draws, but it would take some work, given a) the sheer complexity of
NH, and b) the pure simplicity of the D&D adventure system.

The final product would look more like a hybrid. This board game looks
nice, but can, in no way, capture the randomness of NH with an
unchanging board.

Doing the conversion would be fairly difficult, because you'd have a
lot of issues with room size, given that you'd be using tiles. A
virtual mapping tool could ease that (maptools), but then you just
play NH.

I've been tossing around ideas on how it would work, the sheer time
spent generating a PC would cause severe balance issues. Sure you're
not picking skills, but you still gotta roll stats, and some gear is
randomized. With a meat grinder like NH, you're looking at more of a
randomized Tomb of Horrors than a winnable board game.

I'm rambling now, so tl;dr:
I love the concept, dislike static boards (though would make the trip
back out easier to run), but ultimately, Nethack would be a lot of
work for both the player(s) and a game master, and would still work
better as a tabletop rpg than as a board game


